[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Serverless Architecture in Azure: Functions and Logic Apps"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Building modern cloud-native applications often requires a combination of event-driven compute and orchestration. In Microsoft Azure, Azure Functions provides code-first serverless compute, while Azure Logic Apps offers a low-code, visual workflow engine. Together, they form a powerful toolkit for implementing robust serverless patterns."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites and Core Concepts"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"An active Microsoft Azure subscription."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Azure Functions Core Tools installed locally."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Node.js, C#, or Python runtime environment depending on your function preference."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 1: Creating an HTTP-Triggered Azure Function"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"First, let us create a simple serverless function that acts as an entry point for our workflow. Run the following command in your terminal to initialize a new Azure Functions project."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"func init ServerlessDemo --worker-runtime dotnet-isolated --target-framework net8.0"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Navigate into your project directory and create a new HTTP-triggered function using the command below."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"func new --template "HttpTrigger" --name ProcessOrderFunction"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 2: Implementing Business Logic"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Open the generated function file and update the code to process incoming JSON payloads securely. Ensure you validate the incoming data structure."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"[Function("ProcessOrderFunction")]
public HttpResponseData Run([HttpTrigger(AuthorizationLevel.Function, "post")] HttpRequestData req)
{
    _logger.LogInformation("Processing order execution.");
    var response = req.CreateResponse(HttpStatusCode.OK);
    response.Headers.Add("Content-Type", "application/json; charset=utf-8");
    response.WriteStringAsync("{"status": "Success", "message": "Order received"}");
    return response;
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 3: Orchestrating with Azure Logic Apps"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"While Azure Functions handles custom code execution, Azure Logic Apps orchestrates multiple services without writing extensive boilerplate code. Create a Consumption-based Logic App in the Azure Portal."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Trigger: Choose "When an HTTP request is received" as the workflow trigger."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Action: Add the "Azure Functions" connector and select your newly deployed ProcessOrderFunction."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Condition: Add a conditional control block to check if the function output returns a success status."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Action (True): Send an email notification using Office 365 Outlook or send a message to an Azure Service Bus queue."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion and Best Practices"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining Azure Functions for granular compute tasks and Azure Logic Apps for workflow orchestration, you create a scalable, maintainable serverless architecture. Always implement proper error handling, secure your function endpoints using Function or Managed Identity authorization, and monitor execution metrics via Application Insights."}]
